Opaque data held under an id for a consumer to interpret — the data container, bibliography sources, and datasets.

<section #sh-data | `<data>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *data*. Compiles to eHTML `<data>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<data>`

<code-block #code:sh-data-1>
<data>
  <library format=bibtex>
    @article{goodall2024,
      author = {Goodall, Jane},
      title = {The Effect of Elephants on Climate},
      journal = {Nature},
      year = {2024}
    }
  </library>
</data>
</code-block>

*(Rendered preview omitted — `<data>` / `@`-references resolve at document scope, not inside a sealed preview.)*

*A library block in BibTeX format. The library plugin parses this,
registers entries in the citation system. The &lt;data&gt; block itself
produces no rendered output.*

<section #sh-dataset | `<dataset>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *dataset*. Compiles to eHTML `<dataset>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<dataset>`

<code-block #code:sh-dataset-1>
<data>
  <dataset #sales csv>
quarter,revenue
Q1,100
Q2,120
Q3,145
  </dataset>
</data>

<table src="@sales" />
</code-block>

*(Rendered preview omitted — `<data>` / `@`-references resolve at document scope, not inside a sealed preview.)*

*A CSV dataset is stored once under #sales and a &lt;table src="@sales" /&gt; pulls
it in and renders it as a grid. The dataset itself renders nothing; the
consuming element decides how to present the opaque bytes.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `format` | format-word | `csv` `tsv` `json` `other` | The format word — the canonical way to name the payload language (`&lt;dataset #id csv&gt;…&lt;/dataset&gt;`). |
| `format` | kwarg | `csv` `tsv` `json` `other` | Attribute-form equivalent of the format-word positional (`&lt;dataset #id format=csv&gt;…&lt;/dataset&gt;`). |

<section #sh-library | `<library>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *library*. Compiles to eHTML `<library>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<library>`

<code-block #code:sh-library-1>
<data>
  <library format=bibtex>
    @article{goodall2024,
      author = {Goodall, Jane},
      title = {The Effect of Elephants on Climate},
      journal = {Nature},
      year = {2024}
    }

    @book{darwin1859,
      author = {Darwin, Charles},
      title = {On the Origin of Species},
      publisher = {John Murray},
      year = {1859}
    }
  </library>
</data>
</code-block>

*(Rendered preview omitted — `<data>` / `@`-references resolve at document scope, not inside a sealed preview.)*

*A BibTeX library block in its canonical &lt;data&gt; wrapper (§Placement —
a &lt;library&gt; loads only from inside &lt;data&gt;). The parser reads the
entries and registers goodall2024 and darwin1859 in the citation
registry. Citations elsewhere (e.g., &lt;cite goodall2024&gt;) resolve
against these entries. The block itself produces no rendered output.*

<code-block #code:sh-library-2>
<data>
  <library format=csl-json>
    [
      {
        "id": "goodall2024",
        "type": "article-journal",
        "author": [{"family": "Goodall", "given": "Jane"}],
        "title": "The Effect of Elephants on Climate",
        "container-title": "Nature",
        "issued": {"date-parts": [[2024]]}
      }
    ]
  </library>
</data>
</code-block>

*(Rendered preview omitted — `<data>` / `@`-references resolve at document scope, not inside a sealed preview.)*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `format` | format-word | `bibtex` `csl-json` `ris` `endnote-xml` `other` | The format word — the canonical way to name the payload language (`&lt;library bibtex | …&gt;`). |
| `format` | kwarg | `bibtex` `csl-json` `ris` `endnote-xml` `other` (default `auto`) | Attribute-form equivalent of the format-word positional. |
| `src` | kwarg |  | External source (#133): |
